# LeakSanitizer suppressions for FalkorDB-rs flow tests.
#
# Suppress leaks from C dependencies and Redis itself that are reachable
# at exit by design (long-lived globals, daemons rely on OS to reclaim).
# These are not bugs in falkordb-rs.

# GraphBLAS global workspace and matrices
leak:GB_Global_malloc_function

# Dynamic loader thread-local error buffer
leak:_dlerror_run

# Redis client/server allocations that live until process exit
leak:createRawStringObject
leak:createEmbeddedStringObject
leak:readQueryFromClient
leak:_sdsnewlen

# RediSearch module-level state: scoring/query-expander registries and
# the global trie are allocated at module load and intentionally never freed.
leak:Ext_RegisterScoringFunction
leak:Ext_RegisterQueryExpander
leak:__newTrieMapNode
leak:__trieMapNode_AddChildIdx
leak:__trieMapNode_Split

# RediSearch indexed-document storage: documents persist with their index
# until the index is explicitly dropped. We don't explicitly drop indices on
# Redis shutdown — the process just exits — so all accumulated indexed
# state shows as leaked. Suppress; real-leak detection in this path would
# require explicit index teardown on shutdown.
leak:RediSearch_CreateDocument2
leak:DocTable_Put
leak:RediSearch_CreateIndexOptions

# Per-thread mempool used by RediSearch's RSValue pool (value.c:getPool).
# Allocated lazily via pthread_setspecific with mempool_destroy as the
# TLS destructor. When the process exits via exit(2) without joining the
# threadpool workers, the destructors don't run, so LSan reports the
# pool struct as leaked. Not a real leak.
leak:mempool_new

# Per-thread rwlock context used by RediSearch's RWLOCK macros
# (rwlock.c:RediSearch_GetLockThreadData). Same TLS-destructor pattern
# as mempool_new — not freed when threads are killed at process exit.
leak:RediSearch_GetLockThreadData

# libc thread-local destructor registrations (used by RediSearch's TLS).
leak:__cxa_thread_atexit_impl

# Dynamic linker (ld-linux.so) startup allocations — not freed at exit.
leak:ld-linux-x86-64.so
